.SH "NAME"
pcre2_substring_list_get \- extract all substrings into new memory
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
\f(CR\&#include <pcre2.h>

int\fP \f(CBpcre2_substring_list_get\fP\f(CR(pcre2_match_data *\fP\f(CImatch_data\fP\f(CR,
                             PCRE2_UCHAR ***\fP\f(CIlistptr\fP\f(CR,
                             PCRE2_SIZE **\fP\f(CIlengthsptr\fP\f(CR);\fP
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
This is a convenience function for extracting all the captured substrings after
a pattern match. It builds a list of pointers to the strings, and (optionally)
a second list that contains their lengths (in code units), excluding a
terminating zero that is added to each of them. All this is done in a single
block of memory that is obtained using the same memory allocation function that
was used to get the match data block. The convenience function
\f(CBpcre2_substring_list_free()\fP can be used to free it when it is no longer
needed. The arguments are:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 11
\f(CImatch_data\fP
.ad l
The match data block
.TP 11
\f(CIlistptr\fP
.ad l
Where to put a pointer to the list
.TP 11
\f(CIlengthsptr\fP
.ad l
Where to put a pointer to the lengths, or \f(CBNULL\fP
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
A pointer to a list of pointers is put in the variable whose address is in
\f(CIlistptr\fP. The list is terminated by a \f(CBNULL\fP pointer. If \f(CIlengthsptr\fP is
not \f(CBNULL\fP, a matching list of lengths is created, and its address is placed in
\f(CIlengthsptr\fP. The yield of the function is zero on success or
\f(CBPCRE2_ERROR_NOMEMORY\fP if sufficient memory could not be obtained.
.sp
There is a complete description of the PCRE2 native API in the
\f(CRpcre2api\fP
page and a description of the POSIX API in the
\f(CRpcre2posix\fP
page.